<p>This is a sample article used only to preview the layout of a D-Sci Consulting blog post. The approved text, written or reviewed by the author, replaces it before anything is published.</p>
<p>A first-in-human (FIH) program raises one practical question: which nonclinical evidence is needed before the first dose, and which studies can be avoided because the answer already exists.</p>

<h2 id="what-the-package-answers">What the package needs to answer</h2>
<p>ICH M3(R2) describes the nonclinical safety studies recommended to support human clinical trials. In practice, the package has to answer a short list of questions for the agency:</p>
<ul>
  <li>What is known about the pharmacology of the candidate and its intended target.</li>
  <li>Which toxicity signals the existing data show, and at which exposures.</li>
  <li>How the starting dose and the dose escalation are justified.</li>
  <li>Which gaps remain, and how each one will be addressed.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="start-from-existing-data">Start from the data that already exist</h2>
<p>Before planning any new study, a structured review of the literature, public databases and internal data shows what the evidence already covers.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Evidence source</th><th>What it can support</th><th>Typical gap</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Published literature</td><td>Mechanism and known class effects</td><td>Data on the specific candidate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Public databases</td><td>Hazard information on analogues</td><td>Relevance to the intended use</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Internal data</td><td>Early pharmacology and exposure</td><td>Quality and documentation of older studies</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<aside class="callout">
  <p class="callout__title">Key point</p>
  <p>Every study that the existing evidence can replace or reduce is time and investment saved, as long as the rationale is documented and traceable.</p>
</aside>

<h2 id="where-nams-fit">Where NAMs and in silico approaches fit</h2>
<p>Non-animal methods and in silico approaches are useful where they answer the regulatory question. They are not a shortcut: the value lies in knowing where each approach applies, how to combine them and how to justify the choice.</p>
<blockquote><p>NAMs are not a shortcut. They are strategy.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="documenting-the-rationale">Documenting the rationale</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Map the regulatory requirements for the planned trial.</li>
  <li>Compare them with the available evidence, item by item.</li>
  <li>Define the assays that are truly needed, prioritizing qualified non-animal methods.</li>
  <li>Write the weight-of-evidence rationale, with traceable references.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="when-a-study-is-needed">When a traditional study is still needed</h2>
<p>Not every endpoint can be answered without animal studies today. A sound strategy says so clearly and designs the remaining studies to answer the open questions with the fewest resources.</p>
